<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-1-2-hardware-cycle" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Computer Organization and the Fetch and Execute Cycle</title>

  <p>
    To understand how a computer executes a program, you only need to look at the
    relationship between two primary hardware components: Main Memory (RAM) and the
    Central Processing Unit (CPU). Main memory can be thought of as a vast, ordered
    grid of numbered storage boxes, each identified by a unique numerical address.
    RAM holds both the data your program works with and the step-by-step instructions
    of the program itself while the computer is turned on. The CPU is the processing
    engine of the computer. It contains the electronic circuitry needed to perform
    arithmetic and logical comparisons, along with a small collection of ultra-fast
    internal storage slots called registers used to hold values during immediate
    calculations. The CPU and main memory are connected by a high-speed communication
    channel called the bus, which allows the CPU to read instructions and data from
    memory and write results back.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="figure-cpu-and-memory">
    <caption>The CPU and memory</caption>
    <image source="figures/figure_1_1.png" width="90%">
      <shortdescription>Diagram showing the CPU components connected to addressed main memory via a system bus.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A block diagram titled <q>Computer Architecture: CPU, Bus, and Main Memory.</q> On the left, a container labeled <q>Central Processing Unit (CPU)</q> holds three components linked by internal bidirectional arrows: <q>Registers</q> (listing <c>PC: 0x01</c>, <c>IR: LOAD 0x10</c>, <c>R0: 42</c>, and <c>R1: 15</c>), the <q>Control Unit</q> (labeled <q>Fetches &amp; Decodes Instructions</q>), and the <q>Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</q> (labeled <q>Performs Calculations &amp; Logic</q>). In the center, a thick bidirectional arrow labeled <q>Bus</q> connects the CPU to a right-hand container labeled <q>Main Memory (RAM).</q> Inside RAM is a two-column table of addresses and contents: <c>0x00</c> (<c>LOAD 0x10</c>), <c>0x01</c> (<c>ADD 0x11</c>), <c>0x02</c> (<c>STORE 0x12</c>), <c>0x03</c> (<c>HALT</c>), followed by data values at <c>0x10</c> (<c>42</c>), <c>0x11</c> (<c>15</c>), and <c>0x12</c> (<c>[result]</c>).
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Although modern software can perform sophisticated tasks, the physical CPU is capable
    of executing only its native machine language—a primitive set of instructions encoded
    entirely as binary numbers (sequences of 0s and 1s). Each machine-language instruction
    performs only a microscopic, elementary operation, such as copying a number from a
    memory address into a CPU register, adding two values together, or checking whether a
    result equals zero. Because writing software directly in strings of binary numbers is
    unimaginably tedious and error-prone, programmers write code in high-level programming
    languages (such as Java, C++, or Python) that use readable English words, familiar
    punctuation, and mathematical operators. Before the physical hardware can execute
    this code, a specialized program called a compiler must translate the human-readable
    source code into the binary machine-language instructions that the CPU can directly
    understand.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-machine-language-instructions">
    <title>Example Machine Language Instructions and Assembly Mnemonics</title>
    <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col width="24%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="16%"/>
      <col width="38%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Binary Machine Code</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Assembly Mnemonic</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Operation</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Description</strong></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0001 0001 0110 0100</c></cell>
        <cell><c>LOAD R1, [100]</c></cell>
        <cell>Load</cell>
        <cell>Copy the 8-bit value from memory address 100 into CPU register R1.</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0001 0010 0110 0101</c></cell>
        <cell><c>LOAD R2, [101]</c></cell>
        <cell>Load</cell>
        <cell>Copy the 8-bit value from memory address 101 into CPU register R2.</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0010 0011 0001 0010</c></cell>
        <cell><c>ADD R3, R1, R2</c></cell>
        <cell>Arithmetic</cell>
        <cell>Add the values in R1 and R2 using the ALU; store the sum in R3.</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0011 0011 0110 0110</c></cell>
        <cell><c>STORE R3, [102]</c></cell>
        <cell>Store</cell>
        <cell>Copy the value from register R3 into memory address 102.</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0100 0000 1100 1000</c></cell>
        <cell><c>JUMP [200]</c></cell>
        <cell>Branch</cell>
        <cell>Change the Program Counter (PC) to address 200, jumping execution there.</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><c>0101 0001 1100 1000</c></cell>
        <cell><c>JUMP_IF_ZERO R1, [200]</c></cell>
        <cell>Conditional</cell>
        <cell>If register R1 equals zero, jump to address 200; otherwise continue sequentially.</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Once a compiled program is loaded into memory, the CPU carries it out through a
    continuous process known as the fetch-and-execute cycle (sometimes called the
    instruction cycle). Inside the CPU, a dedicated register called the Program
    Counter (PC) holds the memory address of the next instruction to be run. The cycle
    repeats billions of times every second in three distinct steps:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Fetch:</strong> The CPU retrieves the instruction waiting at the memory
        address currently indicated by the Program Counter and loads it into its internal
        instruction register.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Decode:</strong> The CPU’s control circuitry translates the binary
        instruction to determine what operation needs to be performed (such as an addition,
        memory lookup, or comparison) and what data values are involved.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Execute:</strong> The CPU carries out the operation, updates any affected
        registers or memory addresses, and advances the Program Counter to the address of
        the next sequential instruction.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    This cycle runs without pause until the program explicitly tells the processor to halt.
    By repeating this simple loop of fetching, decoding, and executing primitive machine
    instructions at incomprehensible speeds, the CPU brings complex software to life.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="figure-fetch-decode-execute-cycle">
    <caption>The fetch-decode-execute cycle</caption>
    <image source="figures/figure_1_2.png" width="85%">
      <shortdescription>Cyclic flowchart showing the three stages of the CPU instruction cycle: Fetch, Decode, and Execute.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A clockwise cyclic flowchart titled <q>The CPU Instruction Cycle (Fetch-Decode-Execute)</q> arranged around the center label <q>Continuous Cycle: Repeats billions of times per second.</q> At the top, box <q>1. FETCH</q> explains retrieving the next instruction from RAM at the Program Counter address, with an example showing <c>PC = 0x01 -&gt; Fetch 'ADD 0x11'</c> and <c>PC</c> incrementing to <c>0x02</c>. An arrow leads to the bottom-right box, <q>2. DECODE,</q> explaining that the Control Unit interprets the opcode, with an example showing <c>Opcode: ADD -&gt; Requires ALU addition</c> and <c>Operand: 0x11 -&gt; Load operand value (15)</c>. An arrow leads to the bottom-left box, <q>3. EXECUTE,</q> explaining that the ALU performs the computation and saves the result, with an example showing <c>ALU adds R0 (42) + operand (15) = 57</c> saved in a register/accumulator. A final arrow loops back up to <q>1. FETCH.</q>
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <reading-questions xml:id="checkpoint-1-2">
    <title>Checkpoint</title>

    <exercise xml:id="checkpoint-1-2-hardware-matching" label="checkpoint-1-2-hardware-matching">
      <title>Hardware Components</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Match each hardware component to its role in executing a program.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise order="3">Main Memory (RAM)</premise>
          <response>Stores program instructions and data in addressed slots while the computer is on.</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise order="1">Central Processing Unit (CPU)</premise>
          <response>Fetches, decodes, and executes instructions using arithmetic, logic, and registers.</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise order="4">The Bus</premise>
          <response>Carries instructions and data back and forth between the CPU and main memory.</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise order="2">Program Counter (PC)</premise>
          <response>Holds the memory address of the next instruction to be executed.</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="checkpoint-1-2-compiler" label="checkpoint-1-2-compiler">
      <title>The Role of a Compiler</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why is a compiler needed before a program written in a high-level language can run on a computer’s hardware?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It translates human-readable high-level source code into binary machine-language instructions that the CPU can execute.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The physical CPU only understands its native binary machine language, so a compiler translates high-level code into instructions the hardware can run.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It stores the program’s variables and instructions inside Main Memory (RAM) while the program runs.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. Main Memory (RAM) holds the program and its data during execution, while a compiler performs translation before execution begins.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It connects the CPU to Main Memory so instructions can travel between them at high speed.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The physical channel connecting the CPU and Main Memory is the bus, not the compiler.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It executes each instruction one by one by advancing the Program Counter.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The CPU carries out the fetch-decode-execute cycle and updates the Program Counter during execution.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="checkpoint-1-2-assembly-parsons" label="checkpoint-1-2-assembly-parsons" adaptive="yes">
      <title>Challenge: Pseudo-Assembly</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the pseudo-assembly instructions in <xref ref="table-machine-language-instructions" />, the following four-step program adds the values stored at memory addresses <c>100</c> and <c>101</c> and stores their sum at address <c>102</c>:
        </p>
        <pre>LOAD R1, [100]
LOAD R2, [101]
ADD R3, R1, R2
STORE R3, [102]</pre>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks below to write a similar program that loads the value at address <c>105</c> into <c>R1</c>, loads the value at address <c>106</c> into <c>R2</c>, adds them into <c>R3</c>, and stores the result at memory address <c>107</c>. (You might not need all of the blocks.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <blocks>
        <block order="3">
          <cline>LOAD R1, [105]</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>LOAD R2, [106]</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="5">
          <cline>ADD R3, R1, R2</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="2">
          <cline>STORE R3, [107]</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="4" correct="no">
          <cline>LOAD R3, [107]</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>
  </reading-questions>
</section>
